 Today's newsletter is 858 words, a 3-minute read. 

 📈 The dashboard: The S&amp;P 500 closed up 0.6%.

 🔥 Today's stock spotlight: IBM jumped over 7% in extended trading after the company beat expectations and pointed to AI-related revenue.

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Robotaxis, humanoids and semis, oh my 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Tesla's revenue flattened out in the third quarter, but the company launched robotaxi service and humanoid production while still spending far less than expected.

 Why it matters: Investors are scrutinizing AI outlays, with some concerned about the sustainability of the heavy spending that's propping up the economy .

 💸 Follow the money: Tesla reported $4.7 billion in capex in Q3.

 That was more than double the amount from the same period a year earlier but still far below S&amp;P Capital IQ expectations of $7.8 billion. 🔭 The big picture: Wall Street's view of Tesla is increasingly focused on its AI ambitions, self-driving car plans and energy storage business — and less on the blocking and tackling of selling EVs to individuals.

 Tesla disclosed today that it has begun initial production of the Optimus humanoid robot, which CEO Elon Musk has described as a world-changing product. Tesla also recently began production of the Tesla Semi, launched robotaxi service in Austin, and unveiled a flying version of the new Roadster sports car. What we're watching: Any movement from Tesla or SpaceX on a potential merger after Musk said yesterday on SpaceX's earnings call that the two companies have formed special committees to consider a deal.

 He told investors that he's not allowed to be involved in the talks, but he said he thinks it's "a good idea." Musk said today on Tesla's call, "I think I said everything that should be said yesterday." Go deeper 

 
 
