 2. Meta reports 26% revenue growth for Q3 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Meta CEO Mark Zuckerberg at the Meta Connect developer conference last month. Photo: Andrej Sokolow/picture alliance via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Meta shares were advancing in extended trading after the company reported record revenue for the third quarter, Axios' Pete Gannon writes .

 The company brought in $64.6 billion in sales in the September quarter, a 26% increase over last year. It also gave a better-than-expected forecast, saying it expects Q4 revenue to come in between $73 billion and $76 billion. 🤖 The big picture: That's welcome news for investors, who are looking for evidence that the company's massive spending on AI is translating into sales.

 Meta's earnings report didn't provide much detail on exactly how AI is fueling revenue, or by how much, except that it is "delivering substantial incremental advertising revenue through stronger engagement, better recommendations, and more efficient monetization." 💸 And Meta intends to keep on spending. The company expects capex for the year to be in the range of $135 billion–$145 billion, narrowed from its prior outlook of $130 billion–$145 billion. The intrigue: To help fund that spending, Meta said its board has authorized a new class of publicly listed, nonvoting Class C common stock, which is intended to allow the company to raise needed capital while preserving founder and CEO Mark Zuckerberg's control.

 📈 The impact: Meta shares were trading up 4% after the bell.

 
 
