 4. AI vs. AI 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Artificial intelligence exhibited one of its much talked about use cases today: helping find cybersecurity vulnerabilities that other AI could help exploit, Pete writes .

 Catch up quick: Security researchers are sounding the alarm over two critical vulnerabilities in an open-source programming language known as PHP used to run millions of websites around the world.

 Government officials from the U.S. to Japan are warning companies to apply a patch, or consider taking down their sites to avoid an attack. State of play: OpenAI touted the role its model played in discovering the issue, which allowed PHP researchers to create a patch.

 Now there's a race for anyone using the software in their sites to deploy it, before hackers can use AI models themselves to exploit it. 💭 Our thought bubble, via Axios Future of Cybersecurity author Sam Sabin : "Security researchers have been skeptical of AI-generated vulnerability reports, many of which have turned out to be minor bugs or false alarms. But the PHP discovery could force the cybersecurity community to reckon with the reality of AI threats."

 The bottom line: AI threats beget AI opportunities. "Cybersecurity will likely be the next major use case of AI," Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang told an industry conference in September . 

 "What better way to create demand than to create a problem," he said. Go deeper 

 
 
